The above operation failed in interpreter, with the following stack trace:

cc @ngimel

Introduction
Unrolling and vectorized memory access is beneficial for bandwidth bound kernels, and it is becoming more important. We need unrolling and vectorized memory access to achieve great performance.
Current status of gpu loop
The current design of gpu loop (simplified) works as follows:
template<int nt, int vt, typename func_t>
__global__ void elementwise_kernel(int N, func_t f) {
  int tid = threadIdx.x;
  int nv = nt * vt;
  int idx = nv * blockIdx.x + tid;
  #pragma unroll
  for (int i = 0; i < vt; i++) {
    if (idx < N) {
      // inside f, the address of data is computed, the data is loaded,
      // the computation is done and data is stored back to memory.
      f(idx);
      idx += nt;
    }
  }
}

void gpu_kernel(TensorIterator& iter, const func_t& f) {
  elementwise_kernel<nt, vt, func_t><<<grid, block, 0, stream>>>(N, [=]GPU_LAMBDA(int idx) {
    arg0_t* out = (arg0_t*)(data[0] + strides[0] * idx);
    *out = f(data[1] + strides[1] * idx, data[2] + strides[2] * idx, ...);
  });
}

void add_kernel_cuda(TensorIterator& iter) {
  gpu_kernel(iter, []GPU_LAMBDA(thrust_t a, thrust_t b) -> thrust_t {
    return a + b;
  });
}
The schedule is controlled by elementwise_kernel while all the memory access is done inside a lambda that elementwise_kernel have no knowledge about. And inside the lambda, it does the fetch, compute and store of only a single element. Unfortunately, this design is not very friendly to implementing vectorized load. I am planning to refactor it as the following while doing the work:
__global__ void elementwise_kernel(func_t f, data, strides) {
  #pragma unroll
  compute addresses

  #pragma unroll
  data loading, maybe vectorize

  #pragma unroll
  compute f(...)

  #pragma unroll
  store results
}
this new design would allow us to easily control data loading and storing.
The plan

 Step 0: Move old code to a new namespace legacy, and all new code will go to modern. These two namespaces will coexist for a while and eventually, both of them will go away as we merge them. (#31974)
 Step 1: Write new codes for the case of trivial 1d contiguous problem, supporting unroll. (#31974)
 Step 2: Refactor the code in step 1 to do vectorized load and store (#32383).
 Step 3: Get rid of unnecessary dynamic casting (#32755).
 Step 4: Legacy code cleanup -- gpu_kernel_with_index (#32777).
 Step 5: Migrate torch.where to TensorIterator (#32984).
 Step 6: Support the case where arguments do not have the same dtype (#33553).
 Step 7: Remove gpu_kernel_with_index (#33370).
 Step 8: Template argument cleanup (#33308).
 Step 9: Make ELU great again (#33244).
 Step ..N: Not planned in detail yet

